  "body": "The conjecture misreads the rule. The rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\". A fenced span containing two objects does not parse as one object, so it is passed over; the fence does not \"hold one\" object, and the fallback to the last top-level object is what the rule prescribes. The code returns {'c':3} for both, matching the rule. The reading 41cb58915f3739b9 also overstates by saying \"No uncertainty\" when the conjecture explicitly allows the fallback reading. A next conjecture should test a fence holding exactly one object plus a trailing top-level object, expecting the fenced object, and avoid repeating the claim that a fence with two objects must score the last fenced object.",
